[
{ type: install
  message: <<EOM
World Monitor is installed in %%WWWDIR%%.

1. The file %%PREFIX%%/etc/worldmonitor.env was created from the sample during
   installation and contains only example values.  Generate a secret for
   each of LOCAL_API_TOKEN, WM_SESSION_SECRET,
   UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN and RELAY_SHARED_SECRET, for example with
   "openssl rand -hex 32", and put them into that file.
   Use the same value for WORLDMONITOR_RELAY_KEY as for RELAY_SHARED_SECRET.
   The services refuse to start while a value is empty or still an example
   value (change-me...).

2. Enable and start Redis, the Redis REST proxy, the relay, the local API
   server and the seed service:

     sysrc redis_enable=YES worldmonitor_redis_rest_enable=YES worldmonitor_relay_enable=YES worldmonitor_enable=YES worldmonitor_seed_enable=YES
     service redis start
     service worldmonitor_redis_rest start
     service worldmonitor_relay start
     service worldmonitor start
     service worldmonitor_seed start

3. Serve the frontend with a web server, nginx is not installed as a
   dependency.  Install it, for example with "pkg install nginx", and copy
   %%EXAMPLESDIR%%/nginx-server.conf to
   %%PREFIX%%/etc/nginx/worldmonitor.conf.  The file is a server block for
   port 8080.  Replace change-me-token in it with the value of
   LOCAL_API_TOKEN and make sure only root and nginx can read it.  Then
   add the line

     include worldmonitor.conf;

   inside the http block of %%PREFIX%%/etc/nginx/nginx.conf, enable nginx
   with "sysrc nginx_enable=YES" and start or reload it.  The dashboard is
   then at http://<host>:8080/.

4. The dashboard shows only what is stored in Redis, and that data expires
   after minutes to hours.  The seed service refills it in the background.
   The first run takes a while, follow it in
   /var/log/worldmonitor_seed.log.  Panels that need an API key stay empty
   until you add the key to %%PREFIX%%/etc/worldmonitor.env, the keys are
   listed there.  Most of them are free.  Restart worldmonitor (the local
   API server), worldmonitor_relay and worldmonitor_seed afterwards.

   At least 4 GB of memory are recommended.  On a smaller machine set
   worldmonitor_seed_parallel in /etc/rc.conf to a lower value, for example 2.

   The military bases panel is built by the seed service from OpenStreetMap,
   MIRTA and a curated list.  The first build downloads the data from the
   Overpass API, it is repeated once a month.

5. Redis listens on 127.0.0.1 without a password and without a memory limit,
   so every local user can read it.  All services run as user www.

6. The Pro panels do not work in this port, see the description in
   "pkg info worldmonitor".  To get the
   sign-in buttons, build the port with your own Clerk publishable key:

     echo 'WORLDMONITOR_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_test_...' >> /etc/make.conf

   You must also set CLERK_JWT_ISSUER_DOMAIN in
   %%PREFIX%%/etc/worldmonitor.env.  This only adds a login, the Pro panels
   stay locked.
EOM
},
{ type: upgrade
  message: <<EOM
After an upgrade compare %%PREFIX%%/etc/worldmonitor.env with
%%PREFIX%%/etc/worldmonitor.env.sample, new variables may have been
added, and restart the services:

  service worldmonitor_redis_rest restart
  service worldmonitor_relay restart
  service worldmonitor restart
  service worldmonitor_seed restart
EOM
},
{ type: remove
  message: <<EOM
World Monitor was removed.  These files were left on purpose, delete them
if you do not need them any more:

  %%PREFIX%%/etc/worldmonitor.env      your configuration and secrets
  /var/db/worldmonitor                 generated data, for example the military bases
  /var/log/worldmonitor*.log*          log files

Also remove the worldmonitor_*_enable lines from /etc/rc.conf.  Redis, the
Node.js runtime and curl stay installed, "pkg autoremove" removes them if
nothing else needs them.
EOM
}
]
